A||N||16||bHooper||I need a Input Seal for a Getrag||||1||
Z||000000||bHooper||04-04-2000||10:27 AM||||The shop that is working on my getrag is replaceing all the seals.  Unfortunately we are haveing a time trying to find the Input Seal... this is the one that sits in the bellhouseing and the input shaft protrudes through.  He has called the Chevy dealer, and all aftermarket sources that he is aware of.  I have talked with the Pontiac dealer and we beleive this may be the input bearing sleave shaft... a $45 part.  <P>Does anyone know for sure?  Does anyone know the part number or a reliable source for the actuall part?<P>Thanks,<P>Hoop||||reg||1
Z||000001||88 Database Nut||04-04-2000||01:54 PM||||The part number for the input shaft bearing used to be 14082181.  I think it changed a couple of years ago, but they can look it up from there.  I bought a couple for 88 Getrags about two years ago, but don't have the updated part numbers handy.  I think they are also available from the fierostore.  I assume you have the tranny taken apart, but if not, see Rodney Dickmans website for complete instructions on working on the Getrag (same as whats in the 88 Helms manual).  Also, if this is an from an '88, there are two designs for the clutch fork.  The earlier design (86-part of 88) has a bushing for the lower part of the fork, while the second design has a needle bearing.  The second design should be indicated by the letters "8XPX" on the tranny case.  <P>Good Luck, are you having other work done?  What's it all gonna cost?  I'm got 140k on one car, it's going to need help sooner than later.||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||04-04-2000||02:01 PM||||at first i took the tranny to the shop because it wasnt shifting properly, after i installed the new speedo gear (this tranny came out of a '87 corsica), he fixed that and is replaceing the seals... the tranny has 157000 miles, but looks like it has been rebuilt at some point.<P>thanks for the part number, i will call the pontiac dealer and have him check it.<P><BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000003||88 Database Nut||04-04-2000||02:11 PM||||No Problem.  I Checked the Fierostore's website, they don't have the part.  If all else fails, try carparts.com  They have more than I thought possible at one place, maybe they have one?  Pretty much a dealer item though, and that $45 is about what I remember it costing.  Make sure they put the new one in so that the weep hole is facing towards the ground.  <P>Good Luck Again||||reg||1
Z||000004||88 Database Nut||04-04-2000||02:40 PM||||Good Grief, I checked carparts.com, they have them!  Pretty cheap too.  Only thing about carparts is that they ship by usps, takes about a week to get stuff (no options on shipping).  I'd go with the dealer first though, even if it costs more, and use carparts.com as a second source.  Nothing like pulling the motor out to do another input shaft bearing/seal.||||reg||1
Z||000005||bHooper||04-04-2000||02:40 PM||||i found the part at a Chevy dealer in Winston Salem... $43.40 for the part, new number is 8672128.  However, this part is shaped like 2 cylinders, one on top of the other... with the top housing the bearings.  The seal that I am replaceing in the tranny... is just a seal with bearings, there is no "sleave" attached to it.  It may well be possible that the previouise rebuild was incorect?!  <P>Needless to say, both the mechanic and I are confused.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||88 Database Nut||04-04-2000||02:55 PM||||Actually, the old sleeve is probably still stuck inside your throughout bearing!  The sleeve breaks off and rides on the input shaft, which causes binding (not to mention wearing on the shaft splines).  Could be it was repaired with wrong part before, or just not fixed, or fixed and the sleeve broke again and was lost during the present work.  The new part is just as you described it, sounds like you've got it made.||||reg||1
Z||000007||bHooper||04-04-2000||03:02 PM||||yea, but whats the differance between the input shaft sea and input shaft bearing?<P>the part number you gave me was for the input shaft bearing... right?<P>i think all i need is the input shaft seal....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||88 Database Nut||04-04-2000||03:11 PM||||They are one and the same.  Integrated seal and bearing and sleeve.  I can understand your confusion!  One unit, which you can have pressed in once the tranny has been split (see Rodney's page).   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   ||||reg||1
Z||000009||88 Database Nut||04-04-2000||03:48 PM||||One last thought before I head for home.  All I have said applies to a Fiero Getrag. I'm 99% sure it's also applicable to your tranny, but did you look up this part under parts for an 87 corsica or 86-88 getrag (Muncie 282)?<BR>Better verify you're getting the right part.  For a Fiero Getrag, the sleeve is attached to the front of the bearing, and the seal is internal in the bearing.  The throughout bearing rides on this sleeve, if your current  input shaft bearing doesn't have this sleeve, check the inside of the throughout bearing for one.  Good Luck, I'll check in again tomorrow, or you can call me 2night at (770) 578-6556   John||||reg||1
Z||000010||88 Database Nut||04-04-2000||03:50 PM||||Darnit, THROWOUT Bearing, not throughout.  Long Day...||||reg||1
